#!/usr/bin/python3
"""Change a system setting for the Settings window (flickos-control).

Runs as root through pkexec; polkit asks for an administrator's password
(/usr/share/polkit-1/actions/org.flickos.control.policy, one action per verb).
Only these verbs exist, and every value is checked before anything changes:

  keyboard MODEL LAYOUT VARIANT OPTIONS
      The keyboard of the login screen, the console and new accounts:
      /etc/default/keyboard (keyboard-configuration; keyboard(5)). Each value
      must be in the XKB rules list (evdev.lst); several layouts, variants and
      options are separated by commas. The file's other lines are kept. The
      login screen uses it at its next start, the console at the next boot.

  autologin-on
      Log the calling user (pkexec's PKEXEC_UID, a regular account) in
      without a password once per boot: that user becomes the only member of
      group autologin, whose first member flickos-greeter logs in.

  autologin-off
      The calling user leaves group autologin.

  login-background default|PATH|-
      The login screen's background (flickos-greeter ADR-014): Background= in
      GREETER_CONF, which the login screen reads over flickos-greeter's
      conffile. default drops the line. PATH is a PNG or JPEG under
      /usr/share/backgrounds/ (FlickOS's wallpapers, readable by everyone),
      or a link there into /usr/share/flickos/ (the styles' wallpapers).
      - takes a JPEG of at most BACKGROUND_BYTES on standard input, which
      flickos-control made from a picture of the user's, and writes it to
      LOGIN_BACKGROUND, where the login screen's user can read it. The helper
      never opens a path the caller names outside /usr/share/backgrounds/:
      as root it would copy any file on the computer to a place everyone can
      read. A copy no longer used is deleted.

  login-position left|center|right|default
      Where the login box sits on its screen (flickos-greeter ADR-015):
      Position= in GREETER_CONF; default drops the line.

  user-add NAME FULLNAME ADMIN
      Add a regular account (adduser, so /etc/skel is copied) with the
      password on standard input, in the groups the installer gives the first
      account (DEFAULT_GROUPS), and in group sudo if ADMIN is "yes".

  user-remove NAME FILES
      Remove a regular account (userdel) that is not logged in and not the
      calling user's; FILES "delete" also deletes its home folder, "keep"
      leaves it. Its picture goes either way: userdel leaves it, and a new
      account of that name would show it on the login screen.

  user-admin NAME ADMIN
      Put a regular account in group sudo ("yes") or take it out ("no").

  user-password NAME
      Set a regular account's password to the one on standard input.

  firewall-on
      Turn the ufw firewall on now and at every start (ufw --force enable:
      no question about ssh connections, there is nobody to answer it).

  firewall-off
      Turn the ufw firewall off now and at every start (ufw disable).

  firewall-local-ports off|PORTS
      Let devices on home and office networks (PRIVATE_NETWORKS) reach servers
      you run yourself, through the firewall (docs/design/flickos-dev.md,
      ADR-006): PORTS is a comma-separated list such as 8000/tcp,3000/tcp or
      5000:5010/tcp (a range), each from FIRST_PORT up, with the protocol, at
      most LOCAL_PORTS_MAX for each of tcp and udp. Writes a ufw app profile
      (LOCAL_PORTS_PROFILE) and allows it from those networks only; off deletes
      both. A profile that isn't FlickOS's is left alone.

  containers-dhcp on|off
      Let podman's DHCP helper (netavark) start (flickos-kit-containers keeps
      its three units off until the file NETAVARK_FLAG exists): on creates the
      file and starts the socket, off deletes the file and stops the units. A
      file that isn't FlickOS's is left alone.

  packet-capture on|off
      Let the calling user (PKEXEC_UID, a regular account) capture packets
      without root, Wireshark's own way (docs/design/flickos-security.md,
      ADR-S02): on sets Debian's debconf answer
      wireshark-common/install-setuid to true, reconfigures wireshark-common
      (its postinst gives DUMPCAP, root:wireshark 754, the capture
      capabilities) and adds the user to group wireshark. off takes the user
      out; when no member is left, the answer goes back to false and the
      reconfigure takes the capabilities away. Refused while an
      administrator's dpkg-statoverride is on DUMPCAP (the postinst then
      changes nothing, and the administrator's choice wins). Applies at the
      user's next login.

  wireless-test-adapter ADDRESS|off
      Make NetworkManager leave one Wi-Fi adapter alone, for monitor mode
      without airmon-ng check kill (flickos-security.md, ADR-S08): ADDRESS
      is the permanent address (NetworkManager's PermHwAddress) of a Wi-Fi
      adapter present now and not on a network. Writes TEST_ADAPTER_CONF
      (unmanaged-devices=mac:ADDRESS) and reloads NetworkManager's
      configuration; one adapter at a time. off deletes the file and
      reloads. A file that isn't FlickOS's is left alone.

  upstream ID on|off|refresh
      A maker's own apt repository, for a tool Debian doesn't carry
      (docs/design/flickos-dev.md ADR-008; flickos-security.md ADR-S07): ID
      names an entry of UPSTREAMS, never an address or a key. on writes the
      entry's sources (UPSTREAM_SOURCES, signed by the maker's key that this
      package ships in UPSTREAM_KEYS, refused unless its fingerprint is the
      listed one), a pin that keeps every other package of that origin out
      (UPSTREAM_PINS) and its origin for automatic updates (UPSTREAM_ORIGINS,
      and 60flickos-updates' list), fetches that repository's lists only, and
      installs the entry's packages when apt's simulation removes nothing; if
      a step fails, the files it wrote are deleted again. off removes the
      entry's packages (refused when apt would remove anything else), then
      the files. refresh (postinst) writes the files of the entries that are
      on again from this version's list. Files that aren't FlickOS's are left
      alone.

  firewall-phones on|off|refresh
      Let phones with the KDE Connect app (flickos-phone) through the
      firewall, only from private addresses (PRIVATE_NETWORKS), or stop
      letting them. on writes a ufw app profile (PHONES_PROFILE: TCP 1716
      and 1739-1764, UDP 1716 for a phone's broadcast) and allows it from
      each private range; off deletes those rules and the profile. The
      profile is there exactly while phones are let in, which is how
      flickos-control reads this setting (ufw's own rules need root even to
      read). refresh rewrites an outdated FlickOS profile and its rules
      (ufw app update) and does nothing else; flickos-control's postinst
      runs it, so an upgrade brings new ports to phones already let in.

  firewall-classroom on|off
      Let a teacher's computer reach the classroom agent
      (flickos-classroom-view, TCP CLASSROOM_PORTS) through the firewall,
      only from private addresses, or stop letting it. Like
      firewall-phones: on writes CLASSROOM_PROFILE and allows it from each
      private range, off deletes the rules and the profile, which is there
      exactly while the port is open.

  power SETTING ACTION
      What the power button (SETTING power-key), closing the lid (lid) or
      closing it while plugged in (lid-external-power) does: ACTION poweroff,
      suspend, lock, ignore, or default (logind's own; for the lid while
      plugged in: the same as on battery). Written to a logind drop-in
      (LOGIND_DROP_IN, its other settings kept); logind reloads it at once.

  updates SETTING VALUE | updates refresh
      Automatic updates (unattended-upgrades): SETTING auto (yes, no),
      scope (security, debian: Debian's own choice and FlickOS with its
      community apps, all: also stable-updates) or reboot (no, idle: when
      nobody is logged in); VALUE default drops the choice. Written to
      UPDATES_DROP_IN, which sorts after Debian's 20auto-upgrades and
      50unattended-upgrades. debian and all include the apps bought from the
      FlickOS store (purchases-add), which match nothing without its key.
      refresh writes the drop-in again from its stored choices with this
      version's origins; flickos-control's postinst runs it.

  firefox-memory on|off
      Make Firefox ESR use less memory, for everyone (on), or leave it to
      Firefox's own defaults (off): FIREFOX_PREFS, a file of default prefs
      that Debian's Firefox reads from /etc/firefox-esr/ at its next start.
      The prefs are fixed here (FIREFOX_MEMORY_PREFS); a user's own values
      still win. on needs Debian's firefox-esr; off works without it.

  i386-on
      Let 32-bit packages be installed (Wine's 32-bit part, which every
      Windows installer needs: flickos-switch ADR-004): dpkg
      --add-architecture i386. The next apt update fetches i386's lists too.

  i386-off
      Take i386 away again (dpkg --remove-architecture i386), refused while
      an i386 package that someone asked for is installed (apt's manual
      mark). The ones left are purged first, since dpkg refuses to drop an
      architecture while any is known: libraries only installed for others
      (apt never autoremoves libgcc-s1, which is protected, nor what it
      needs) when apt would remove nothing else with them, and ones only
      their config files are left of. apt counts libgcc-s1:i386 and what it
      needs as essential, so the purge allows that, once its simulation shows
      that only i386 packages go.

  locale-add LOCALE
      Make a language available: LOCALE must be a UTF-8 locale in
      /usr/share/i18n/SUPPORTED. Its line in /etc/locale.gen is turned on
      (or added), and locale-gen generates the locales that are missing.

  locale NAME=VALUE...
      The language and formats of the login screen, the console and new
      accounts: /etc/locale.conf (/etc/default/locale is a link to it in
      trixie; if it is a file of its own, it is written too). NAME is LANG
      (required), LANGUAGE or an LC_ variable, each at most once; VALUE a
      locale of /usr/share/i18n/SUPPORTED, C, C.UTF-8 or POSIX (LANGUAGE: a
      colon-separated list of names). Debian's systemd refuses localed's
      SetLocale to everyone (systemd-localed-read-only.conf), so `localectl
      set-locale` can't do it.

  packages-undo ID
      Undo one change to the installed software (Settings > History): the
      apt transaction whose Start-Date is ID (YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS), read from
      apt's own log here, never from the caller. An install is undone by
      removing the packages the user asked for, with the ones it brought in
      unless another package needs them; a removal by installing them again.
      apt simulates it first, and nothing happens if it would also remove
      another package, anything the FlickOS desktop depends on, or install a
      live-session package, or if the transaction was part of installing the
      system. Updates can't be undone (Debian keeps only the newest version).

  purchases-add
      Add the key of apps bought from the FlickOS store, given on standard
      input (never in the arguments, where ps shows it): the key's format is
      checked, then the store says what it holds (/v1/key). A valid key
      already on the computer stays, and the store links the new one to it
      (/v1/link), so apt's one login per address holds every purchase; one
      the store turned off is replaced. Writes apt's login (STORE_AUTH, root
      0600), the store's sources entry (STORE_SOURCES, signed by FlickOS's
      key), PURCHASES_FILE (what the key holds, without the key), updates the
      store's lists and runs purchases-check. Refused in the live session.

  purchases-remove
      Delete those files and the pins. Installed apps stay installed.

  purchases-check
      Keep the store's packages this computer's key can't download at their
      installed version (STORE_PINS, Pin-Priority -1), so one refunded app
      never stops the automatic updates (unattended-upgrades aborts its whole
      run on a download it can't make). Asks the store at most every
      CHECK_SECONDS; no answer changes nothing. Run as root by apt after
      every update (flickos-control's 61flickos-purchases), only while a key
      is on the computer; not through pkexec.

Two verbs only read, and run without root (and without pkexec), so that the
Settings window shows the same list and plan the undo will use:

  packages-list [DAYS]
      The software changes of the last DAYS days (90) as JSON, oldest first,
      without the ones made while installing the system.

  packages-plan ID
      What packages-undo ID would do, as JSON, or why it can't.

The last administrator (member of group sudo, which polkit and sudo ask for)
can't be removed or made a standard user.

Exit codes: 0 ok, 1 error, 2 usage error.
"""

